// One walk over the expression tree — the first consumer a brace pattern has ever had.
//
// `sideEffectFree` is a real question a compiler asks: may this expression be evaluated twice, or
// dropped, or hoisted? It is chosen here because it has to look at **every** variant, so every arm
// is written and the pattern is exercised rather than demonstrated.
//
// Read `ast.wac` first. The short version: an enum payload has field names and no way to use them,
// where a struct's are named, order-independent and omittable when nullable.

import { Vec } from "core";
import { Expr, ExprKind, Ty, Arm, JsxAttr } from "./ast.wac";

/**
 * Whether evaluating this twice is the same as evaluating it once.
 *
 * Every arm below binds by name. Four of them bind a *subset* — which is the thing the positional
 * form cannot do at all, and which is why `{ .. }` earns its place separately from the pattern:
 * `Cast { operand, .. }` says *the target type does not matter here*, and `Cast(_, operand, _)`
 * says the same thing in a spelling where the reader counts underscores.
 */
export bool sideEffectFree(Expr e) {
  match (e.kind) {
    IntLit { .. }:   { return true; }
    FloatLit { .. }: { return true; }
    StrLit { .. }:   { return true; }
    Ident { .. }:    { return true; }

    // `left` and `right` are both `Expr` and the order does not matter to this answer — which is
    // exactly why the positional form is safe *here* and unsafe two functions away. A pattern that
    // is only wrong sometimes is the kind nothing catches.
    Binary { left, right }: { return sideEffectFree(left) && sideEffectFree(right); }
    Unary { operand }:      { return sideEffectFree(operand); }
    Cast { operand, .. }:   { return sideEffectFree(operand); }
    Index { arr, index }:   { return sideEffectFree(arr) && sideEffectFree(index); }

    // The seventeen-arm case. `cond`, `then` and `els` are three `Expr` in a row; written this way
    // a reader does not have to know which of the three came second.
    Ternary { cond, then, els }: {
      return sideEffectFree(cond) && sideEffectFree(then) && sideEffectFree(els);
    }

    // A call is the whole reason this function exists.
    Call { .. }: { return false; }

    // Markup builds a tree and evaluates its holes; the tags cannot do anything.
    Jsx { kids, attrs }: {
      for (Expr k in kids.items())    { if (!sideEffectFree(k)) { return false; } }
      for (JsxAttr a in attrs.items()) { if (!attrValueFree(a)) { return false; } }
      return true;
    }
    JsxText { .. }: { return true; }

    MatchExpr { subject, arms }: {
      if (!sideEffectFree(subject)) { return false; }
      for (Arm a in arms.items()) { if (!armFree(a)) { return false; } }
      return true;
    }
  }
}

bool attrValueFree(JsxAttr a) { … }
bool armFree(Arm a) { … }

// ## What writing it found
//
// **The subset form is the whole value, and the proposal leads with the wrong half.**
// `../../GRAMMAR.ebnf` writes the pattern as `Ok { v }` — every field, by name — with `{ .. }` as an
// afterthought meaning *ignore the rest*. Of the thirteen arms above, **six** bind a subset and
// could not be written positionally without counting underscores: `Cast { operand, .. }` against
// `Cast(_, operand, _)`, and every `{ .. }`-only arm against `IntLit(_)`.
//
// Positional wildcards do exist — `[§wac-arm-partial]` allows ignoring one field at a time — so
// naming a subset is not *new capability*, it is the same capability without an index. That is a
// weaker claim than the proposal makes and it is the one the arms support.
//
// **And the strong claim is elsewhere.** `StructDecl` has seven fields and fifty-four arms in the
// real compiler; almost none of them want all seven. `StructDecl { nameTok, fields, methods, .. }`
// is three names against `StructDecl(nameTok, _, fields, methods, _, _, _)` — four underscores whose
// count is load-bearing, in a variant that has gained a field twice.
//
// ## What could not be written
//
// **A binding that renames.** `Binary { left: lhs, right: rhs }` is not in the grammar —
// `field_pattern = ".." | IDENT , { "," , IDENT } , …` takes bare names only, so a payload field's
// name becomes a local's name whether the local wants it or not. Two nested arms binding `left` from
// different subjects cannot both be written. The struct-literal form it borrows from has `x: 3`, so
// the colon is free notation on the pattern side and the grammar declines it. Not obviously wrong —
// a renaming pattern is a second thing to learn — but it is the difference between *destructuring*
// and *naming*, and the file that proposes it says nothing either way.
//
// **A nested pattern.** `Binary { left: Ident { tok } }` would let one arm reach two levels.
// `GRAMMAR.md` records this as considered and withdrawn: *"a field access that already exists"* says
// the same thing. Fair for reading a field; it is not the same for *matching* one, since the nested
// form is what makes an arm apply only when the inner variant matches. Withdrawn on the reading
// argument, and the matching argument was not made.
//
// **`match` on `e.kind` where the variants are `ExprKind`'s** reads as though the arms were `Expr`'s.
// The subject is a field and the arms name its type's variants, so nothing in the arm says which of
// the two types it belongs to. That is true of the shipped code as well and is not a vision
// question — but a brace pattern makes it worse by looking like a struct pattern on `Expr`.
